.TH PS 1 "November 17, 1996"
.UC
.SH NAME
ps \- process status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
[
.B \-aAdefl
] [
.B \-g
.I grouplist
] [
.B \-G
.I grouplist
] [
.B \-n
.I namelist
] [
.B \-o
.I format
]... [
.B \-p
.I proclist
] [
.B \-t
.I termlist
] [
.B \-u
.I userlist
] [
.B \-U
.I userlist
] [
.B \-w
]
.br
.B ps
[
.B acglnuwx
] [
.BI t tty
] [
.I pid
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ps
reports the processes of the system from the kernel's process records,
which
.IR sysctl (3)
returns for
.BR KERN_PROC .
The kernel fills a record wherever the process image lives \- in memory,
on the swap device or in the SwapRAM pool \- so
.I ps
reads no memory device and needs no privilege.
A process whose record the kernel could only partly fill still has its
row, and each field it could not fill prints as `\-'.
.PP
An argument list that begins with `\-' selects the POSIX grammar;
otherwise the first argument is a 2.11BSD option string.
.SS POSIX options
With no selection option,
.I ps
lists the processes with the caller's effective user ID and controlling
terminal.
The selection options combine by inclusive OR.
.TP
.B \-a
Processes with a controlling terminal.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-e
All processes.
.TP
.B \-d
All processes except process group leaders.
The kernel keeps no session ID, so the process group leader stands in
for the session leader.
.TP
.B \-f
Full listing: UID (as a user name), PID, PPID, C, STIME, TTY, TIME, CMD
(the command and its arguments).
.TP
.BI \-g " grouplist"
Processes whose process group IDs are in
.IR grouplist ,
in place of the session leaders POSIX names.
.TP
.BI \-G " grouplist"
Processes whose real group IDs are in
.IR grouplist .
.TP
.B \-l
Long listing: F, S, UID, PID, PPID, C, PRI, NI, ADDR, SZ, WCHAN, TTY,
TIME, CMD; with
.BR \-f ,
UID is a user name and STIME is added.
.TP
.BI \-n " namelist"
Accepted and ignored: no namelist takes part in the listing.
.TP
.BI \-o " format"
The columns named in
.IR format ,
separated by blanks or commas, in that order.
A name followed by `=' takes the rest of the argument as its header.
When every header is empty, no header line is printed.
.TP
.BI \-p " proclist"
Processes whose IDs are in
.IR proclist .
.TP
.BI \-t " termlist"
Processes whose controlling terminal is in
.IR termlist ,
named as in
.I /dev
(tty0, console) or by the part after `tty' (0).
.TP
.BI \-u " userlist"
Processes whose effective user IDs or login names are in
.IR userlist .
.TP
.BI \-U " userlist"
Processes whose real user IDs or login names are in
.IR userlist .
.TP
.B \-w
A line width of at least 132 columns; twice, no limit.
.PP
The lists are single arguments, separated by blanks or commas.
.SS BSD options
Without options,
.I ps
lists the caller's processes on the caller's terminal, leaving out the
children of init and login shells.
.TP
.B a
Processes of all users.
.TP
.B c
The command name in place of the arguments.
.TP
.B g
Children of init and login shells as well.
.TP
.BR l ", " n
Long listing: F, S, UID, PID, PPID, CPU, PRI, NICE, ADDR, SZ, WCHAN, TT,
TIME, COMMAND.
.TP
.BI t tty
Processes on terminal
.IR tty ,
named as in
.B \-t
or as `co' for the console; alone, the caller's terminal.
It must be the last option.
.TP
.B u
User listing: USER, PID, NICE, SZ, TT, TIME, COMMAND.
.TP
.B w
A line width of at least 132 columns; twice, no limit.
.TP
.B x
Processes without a terminal as well, and the processes
.B g
adds.
.TP
.I pid
That process alone.
It must be the last option.
.PP
The output is sorted by terminal, then by process ID.
.SS Format names
.ta \w'cputime\ \ 'u +\w'ELAPSED\ \ 'u
.nf
Name	Header	Content
args	COMMAND	command and arguments
comm	COMMAND	command name
etime	ELAPSED	time since start, [[dd\-]hh:]mm:ss
group	GROUP	effective group
nice	NI	nice value
pcpu	%CPU	CPU time over elapsed time, percent
pgid	PGID	process group ID
pid	PID	process ID
ppid	PPID	parent process ID
rgroup	RGROUP	real group
ruser	RUSER	real user
time	TIME	CPU time, [dd\-]hh:mm:ss
tty	TT	controlling terminal
user	USER	effective user
vsz	VSZ	data and stack, KiB
.fi
.PP
This system adds:
.PP
.nf
addr	ADDR	`\-': no address is reported
c	C	scheduler CPU usage
cputime	TIME	CPU time, mmm:ss
f	F	process flags, octal (sys/proc.h)
pri	PRI	priority; high numbers mean low priority
s	S	state: S sleeping, W waiting, R running,
		I intermediate, Z exited, T stopped
stime	STIME	start, hh:mm today or MonDD before
sz	SZ	data, stack and user area, KiB
tt	TT	terminal after `tty', `co' for the console
uid	UID	effective user ID
wchan	WCHAN	`\-': no wait channel is reported
.fi
.DT
.PP
Only
.B args
and
.B comm
contain blanks.
.PP
An exited process its parent has not waited for is marked <defunct>.
The arguments are the first 51 bytes of the argument list the process
was started with, which exec records; a process that changes its own
arguments does not change them here.
The kernel returns them only to the super-user and to a caller whose
effective user ID is the process's real or effective user ID; for any
other process, and for a process with no arguments, the command name
prints in brackets.
A byte outside printable ASCII prints as `?'.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The line width, in place of the terminal's.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone of STIME.
.SH FILES
.ta 2i
/dev	searched for terminal names
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kill(1), w(1), sysctl(3)
.SH STANDARDS
The POSIX grammar follows the XSI
.I ps
of IEEE Std 1003.1-2017 and 1003.1-2024, except that
.B \-d
and
.B \-g
use process groups in place of sessions.
.SH BUGS
Things can change while
.I ps
is running; the picture it gives is only a close
approximation to reality.
